{"question_id": "06f04340", "question_type": "single-session-preference", "response": "Based on your garden (cherry tomatoes you've already harvested, plus the basil and mint you've been cooking with), a good weekend dinner would be:\n\n- **Main: Pesto Pasta** — basil blended with garlic, pine nuts, Parmesan and olive oil, tossed with linguine, cherry tomatoes and grilled chicken. It puts your homegrown tomatoes and basil to work in one filling dish.\n- **Starter or side: Caprese Salad** — fresh mozzarella, juicy tomatoes and basil leaves with olive oil and balsamic glaze — or the **Middle Eastern-Style Salad** (basil and mint with bulgur, cherry tomatoes, cucumbers, feta, lemon-tahini dressing) if you want to use the mint as well.\n- **Drink: Herby Lemonade** — lemonade infused with fresh basil and mint leaves — or a **Mint Julep** if you'd prefer a cocktail.\n\nIf your pepper plants start producing despite the aphids, the **Thai-Style Basil and Mint Stir-Fry** would be a good way to use them later.", "n_records": 64, "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 2794, "completion_tokens": 1587, "calls": 1}, "selected": ["self|recipe_preferences", "self|cooking_habits", "self|garden_plants", "self|garden_harvest", "self|pest_problem", "self|pest_control_attempts", "self|gardening_interest", "self|pest_control_plans", "assistant|recipe_suggestion", "assistant|companion_plant_suggestion", "assistant|companion_plant_warning", "assistant|gardening_tip", "assistant|pest_control_suggestion", "assistant|pest_control_opinion"], "digest_bytes": 8000}
{"question_id": "38146c39", "question_type": "single-session-preference", "response": "Given your sugar experiments, a few directions that fit your taste for richer, more intense flavors:\n\n- **Swap in turbinado sugar.** You found it adds a richer flavor than other sugars, and it pairs especially well with chocolate — cocoa powder or dark chocolate builds a rich, indulgent profile with it. Try replacing part of the granulated sugar (the same 1/4 to 1/2 replacement approach you're using for carrot cake works well as a starting point).\n- **Add a pinch of flaky sea salt.** Salt enhances turbinado sugar's flavor, and it's a classic lift for chocolate chip cookies.\n- **Try nuts.** Almonds, pecans, or hazelnuts all pair well with turbinado sugar and complement the cookie's texture.\n- **Consider espresso or coffee.** Its bold flavor pairs beautifully with turbinado sugar in mocha-style desserts — a small amount of espresso powder deepens the chocolate without making the cookie taste like coffee.\n\nIf you want a more caramel-forward cookie, muscovado is another option — it has a stronger molasses/caramel flavor and more moisture than turbinado, though you'd want to keep it to a modest replacement ratio and expect a softer, darker cookie.", "n_records": 26, "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 1792, "completion_tokens": 583, "calls": 1}, "selected": ["self|sugar_experimentation", "self|turbinado_sugar_opinion", "self|carrot_cake_plan", "self|frosting_plan", "self|flavor_preference", "assistant|turbinado_pairing_spices", "assistant|turbinado_pairing_citrus", "assistant|turbinado_pairing_nuts", "assistant|turbinado_pairing_coffee", "assistant|turbinado_pairing_vanilla", "assistant|turbinado_pairing_chocolate", "assistant|turbinado_pairing_dried_fruit", "assistant|turbinado_pairing_coconut", "assistant|turbinado_pairing_ginger", "assistant|turbinado_pairing_salt", "assistant|turbinado_cakes", "assistant|turbinado_cake_tips", "assistant|carrot_cake_turbinado_method", "assistant|carrot_cake_complements", "assistant|carrot_cake_variations", "assistant|turbinado_frosting_tip", "assistant|caramel_frosting_method", "assistant|muscovado_properties", "assistant|demerara_properties", "assistant|muscovado_frosting_tip", "assistant|carrot_cake_nuts"], "digest_bytes": 4014}
{"question_id": "505af2f5", "question_type": "single-session-preference", "response": "Based on your current base recipe (almond milk, vanilla extract, honey) and your goals of cutting sugar and saving money, here are some ways to switch it up:\n\n**Swap the milk for a creamier texture**\n- Cashew milk or oat milk — both are creamier than almond milk and work well in homemade creamer.\n\n**Lower-sugar sweeteners (to replace the honey)**\n- Stevia, monk fruit sweetener, or yacon syrup — all lower-sugar options that still give you sweetness.\n\n**Add spices for flavor without sugar**\n- Cinnamon, nutmeg, or cardamom — each adds warmth and depth (plus some health benefits) with no added sugar.\n\n**Boost the nutrition**\n- Collagen peptides, protein powder, or MCT oil — great for extra energy, especially with your afternoon cravings.\n\n**Two combo ideas:**\n1. *Cozy Spice Creamer*: Oat milk + vanilla + cinnamon + cardamom, sweetened with monk fruit.\n2. *Energy Boost Creamer*: Cashew milk + vanilla + collagen peptides + a touch of stevia.\n\nSince you're already making your own granola and creamer at home, these variations should be easy to mix into your routine. Would you like more detail on any of these?", "n_records": 26, "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 1744, "completion_tokens": 868, "calls": 1}, "selected": ["self|sugar_reduction_goal", "self|money_saving_goal", "self|homemade_creamer", "self|granola_experimentation", "self|homemade_granola_batch", "self|granola_snacking_habit", "self|afternoon_sweet_cravings", "self|breakfast_skipping", "self|weekly_snack_prep_plan", "self|on_the_go_snack_needs", "assistant|creamer_sweetener_alternatives", "assistant|creamer_spice_additions", "assistant|creamer_milk_alternatives", "assistant|creamer_nutrition_boosters", "assistant|sunday_snack_prep_ideas", "assistant|granola_grain_options", "assistant|granola_variation_cinnamon_apple", "assistant|granola_variation_tropical", "assistant|granola_variation_cocoa_crunch", "assistant|on_the_go_snack_brands", "assistant|on_the_go_snack_ideas", "assistant|craving_hydration_tip", "assistant|craving_sleep_tip", "assistant|craving_meal_regularity_tip", "assistant|breakfast_ideas", "assistant|breakfast_nutrition_guidance"], "digest_bytes": 4187}
{"question_id": "fca70973", "question_type": "single-session-preference", "response": "Based on your interests, here's what I'd suggest for your next theme park weekend:\n\n**Top pick: Universal Studios Hollywood during Halloween Horror Nights** (runs mid-September through early November). You've already flagged interest in the VIP Experience's Gourmet Buffet and the Behind-the-Scenes Tour, and together they hit all three of your interests — thrill rides and haunted houses, unique food, and nighttime shows — in one evening:\n\n- **VIP Experience** (~$250–$400+ per person): priority entry, front-of-line access to all mazes and attractions, an exclusive VIP lounge with complimentary snacks and a private bar, a commemorative photo, and a special VIP gift like a limited-edition pin.\n- **Gourmet Buffet**: savory meats, seafood, vegetarian options, and decadent desserts.\n- **Behind-the-Scenes Tour add-on**: ~1 hour in the early evening before the event opens, covering the makeup/FX department, maze design and construction, scare zone design, costumes, and backstage areas — small group size for an intimate experience.\n- Other add-ons: a private meet-and-greet with Halloween Horror Nights' creators and designers, and VIP parking near the entrance.\n\nOne practical note: VIP packages sell out quickly, especially during peak season, so book ahead — online at universalstudioshollywood.com (Tickets > VIP Experiences) or by phone at (818) 622-8477. Also be aware cancellations or changes may incur fees.\n\n**Alternatives if you want variety:** since you've recently visited all four parks, a Halloween-season return to any of them would be a different experience — Disneyland Halloween Time (mid-September, with Oogie Boogie Bash), Knott's Scary Farm, or Six Flags Fright Fest (late September–early November) all offer themed food and nighttime entertainment you wouldn't have seen on your earlier visits.\n\nWould you like more detail on any of these, or help comparing options?", "n_